              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "Despite his name, Flounder is **not actually a flounder**. Real flounders are flat, brown, bottom-dwelling fish with both of their eyes on one side of their head. \n\nBecause his design differs so much from a real flounder, his actual species depends on which version of *The Little Mermaid* you are looking at:\n\n### 1. The 1989 Animated Film (An Angelfish / Generic Reef Fish)\nIn the original animated movie, Flounder is depicted as a chubby, bright yellow and blue tropical fish. Disney animators did not base him on one specific, real-world species, but marine biologists and fans generally agree that his body shape and vibrant coloring most closely resemble a **blue angelfish** or a **royal angelfish**. \n\n### 2. The Animated TV Series (A \"Guppy\")\nIn the lore of *The Little Mermaid* animated television series, Flounder is repeatedly referred to as a **guppy**. This is also a nod to the original film, where Ariel playfully tells him, *\"Don't be such a guppy,\"* and he defensively replies, *\"I'm not a guppy!\"* In the sequel film, *The Little Mermaid II: Return to the Sea*, his offspring are officially called \"Flounder's guppies\". \n\n### 3. The 2023 Live-Action Remake (A Sergeant Major)\nFor the 2023 live-action adaptation, Disney chose to base Flounder's photorealistic look on a real-world fish species: the **sergeant major** (*Abudefduf saxatilis*). This is a type of colorful damselfish native to the Atlantic Ocean that features a rounder body with yellow-and-blue vertical stripes.",
